## Framing challenge first

The brief bundles one safe decision with two unsafe ones. Extending the closed vocabulary is nearly free: `classes.rs` already maps anything unknown to `other`/`unknown`, so new values never break old intakes. The risky parts are (a) committing to a *published score* before the denominators are comparable, and (b) assuming the hook can see effort.

Options the brief does not list:

- **(5) Tier-split panels as the experiment.** Alternative 2 (local A/B) is dismissed as "nothing learned for other users" — but the routed panel already exists. Send the same brief at two efforts to two seats of one panel; the existing consult events capture it for everyone once `effort` ships. This is alternative 1 and 2 combined, at panel cost.
- **(6) Publish counts, keep scores local.** `c3 scoreboard` is already local. Ship only `effort` on events and let each install compute its own usefulness-per-cost; the intake publishes distributions, not rankings. Defers the irreversible "published metric" decision until the findings join (alternative 3) exists.
- **(7) Record effort now, commit to no score at all.** Schema first, semantics later.

What I would need to choose: does z.ai expose more than three glm-5.3 tiers; does `SubagentStop` stdin (or the transcript metadata) carry the Agent call's effort override; how is the intake schema versioned today.

## Q1 — tiers and denominator

Tiers: gpt-6-astra (window-bound): chore low, checkpoint/reading medium, diff-review/acceptance high, core-contract/stuck xhigh. glm-5.3 (money-bound, no 5-hour window): chore low, everything else high, stuck max — the asymmetry is real and the policy should encode it. Denominator: findings-confirmed per unit of the *binding constraint* — window-share for OpenAI, money for GLM, wall time only as a guard. Finding F1: window share is not a cross-vendor denominator (quota.rs reads Codex rollouts only), so "usefulness per window share" cannot be the single published score. Design not listed: score the **marginal finding** — findings no other panel seat produced — per cost unit; it survives routed panels where seats differ.

## Q2 — worker capture

`SubagentStop` is the right capture point for Claude Code; other hosts report via the coordinator's own `rate` path with tier `other`. Must stay out: transcript/cwd/repo paths, session id (hash it), the agent label as typed. Effort: do not trust the transcript — the coordinator knows effort at spawn; have it write a local worker-id→{tier,effort} registry the hook reads (zero tokens, no parsing). Attribution under rework: add outcome `reworked`, marked at rating time; credit completion to the last worker, charge the rework against the original tier's score. Without this the original tier's completion share is inflated (F5).

## Q3 — add to the vocabulary now

`effort` (sent, closed none|low|medium|high|xhigh|max), `effort_requested` (preset name or `other`), `effort_native` (pre-mapping label — otherwise medium→high is indistinguishable from native high later, F2), `cost_basis` (window|money|none) so denominators stay interpretable, and `schema_v` per event. Worker event: tier, effort, outcome class, duration/token buckets, verified flag, resume count, reworked flag.

## Q4 — scout and judge

Failure modes: the scout summarizes the anomaly away; the judge anchors on the scout's conclusion; a cold-cache digest silently drops context. Rules: the scout emits a **fixed schema**, never a verdict — file list, hunk ids, suspicion 0–3 per hunk — capped at ~200 lines; the judge always gets the raw file list and may fetch the raw diff of any hunk (a tool call, not a bigger digest). If the judge cannot fetch raw context, the routing saves money by silently lowering review quality — that is the real cost cap to set.

### Findings

- **F03-1** [major] `crates/c3-core/src/quota.rs:401` - Window-share is not a cross-vendor scoring denominator: quota.rs reads Codex CLI rollouts only, so GLM/z.ai tiers have no window share and 'usefulness per 5-hour-window share' cannot be the single published tier score; a GLM max round looks infinitely cheap on that metric. Trigger: Publishing the intake's usefulness table split per reviewer x effort with window share as the denominator, once GLM events carry effort. Evidence: read-code: All quota observation paths parse Codex rollout token_count lines and Codex session files; no z.ai or money-cost source exists.; read-code: The brief itself states a GLM round at max 'has no 5-hour window', confirming the denominator is OpenAI-only. Verify: Attempt to compute a window-share cost for one glm-5.3 ledger entry; observe that no quota record exists for a non-Codex engine. Remedy: Score per binding constraint with a cost_basis field on the event (window|money|none); publish per-basis tables, never a merged ranking; long-term use marginal-findings-confirmed per cost unit.
- **F03-2** [minor] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:186` - Recording only the mapped effort (medium->high, xhigh->max) plus the preset name loses the native requested tier; later analysis cannot distinguish 'medium requested, sent as high' from 'high natively', biasing the z.ai medium tier's measured usefulness to zero samples forever. Trigger: Any retrospective that tries to evaluate the medium->high mapping itself from intake data. Evidence: read-code: The sketch carries effort (tier sent after mapping) and effort_requested (preset name); neither is the endpoint-native tier label before the vocabulary mapping. Verify: Check whether the consult event constructors have any field for the pre-mapping effort label; they do not. Remedy: Add effort_native (closed list plus other/unknown) alongside effort and effort_requested before the schema is published.
- **F03-3** [major] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:118` - The worker-telemetry design is gated on an unverified assumption that the SubagentStop hook can see the worker's effort; the state notes admit an Agent-call effort override may not appear in the subagent transcript, so the primary tier x effort table could silently record effort=unknown for the majority of workers. Trigger: Shipping the hook without a spawn-time effort source; the intake's workers table aggregates mostly unknown-effort rows. Evidence: read-code: States the override 'may not be in the subagent transcript - an open question for the consilium; fallback: the coordinator's rate command carries --effort'. Verify: Run one Claude Code subagent with an explicit effort override and inspect the SubagentStop stdin JSON and the transcript for the effort value. Remedy: Coordinator-side spawn registry: the coordinator writes worker-id -> {tier, effort} locally at spawn; the hook reads classes from it; keep --effort on the rate command only as backfill.
- **F03-4** [minor] (no location) - The scout-and-judge routing fails by anchoring: if the scout's digest contains a conclusion ('looks fine'), the judge's defect-finding rate drops toward the scout's, and the window saving is bought with judge quality while telemetry credits the judge's tier. Trigger: A digest that hides or soft-pedals the one anomalous hunk (the wave-8 history shows exactly one real defect per round - the digest only has to miss one). Evidence: read-code: The sketch specifies a digest plus a call cap for the judge but no rule that the digest carry only facts or that the judge can fetch raw context.; inferred: With ~1 real defect per round, single-hunk recall is the entire value of the review; a lossy digest destroys it. Verify: Replay one past round (e.g. F60-1) through a scout digest and check whether the judge can still find the defect from the digest alone. Remedy: Fixed-schema digest (file list, hunk ids, suspicion 0-3, no verdict), ~200-line cap, and a judge-side raw-diff fetch per hunk; log digest-coverage in telemetry.
- **F03-5** [note] (no location) - The worker outcome classes (reported|stopped-by-coordinator|error|unknown) have no reworked state; when the coordinator redoes a worker's output, the wave's success attributes to the original tier and inflates its measured completion share. Trigger: Any wave where the coordinator rewrites a worker's patch before the reviewer accepts it (the AGENTS.md delegation guidance already anticipates rework). Evidence: read-code: The outcome class list contains no rework attribution; the attribution question is explicitly open. Verify: Inspect one wave in .collab where the coordinator edited a worker's output and check what the proposed event would have recorded. Remedy: Add outcome reworked, set by the coordinator at rating time; credit completion to the final worker, charge the rework count to the original tier's score.

