### Findings

- **F04-1** [major] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/01-claude-measure-reviewers-and-workers.md:59`, `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:128` - The two planning sources define different closed worker-outcome vocabularies (handoff: reported|stopped-by-coordinator|error|unknown; state.md: completed|error:rate_limit|error:context|error:other|stopped). A closed vocabulary that ships from contradictory sketches will be wrong for one of them and unchangeable once published. Trigger: Implementing worker telemetry from either document without reconciling the outcome class list. Evidence: read-code: outcome class listed as reported | stopped-by-coordinator | error | unknown; read-code: outcome listed as completed, error:rate_limit, error:context, error:other, stopped. Verify: Grep both documents for the outcome lists and diff them; confirm no third source already picks one. Remedy: Adopt the finer list (completed|stopped|error:rate_limit|error:context|error:other|unknown): error cause is the tier signal; map reported->completed, stopped-by-coordinator->stopped; write it into the schema doc before any code.
- **F04-2** [major] `crates/c3/src/telemetry/classes.rs:870`, `crates/c3/src/telemetry/classes.rs:922`, `crates/c3/src/telemetry/event.rs:58` - The client-side closer rebuilds every event from a fixed key set and discards any event_type other than consultation|rating (close_event returns None). A new worker event, and new fields such as effort on existing events, are silently dropped on old or un-updated code paths unless the closer is extended in the same release with additive semantics (absent new fields must still close). Trigger: Queueing a worker event or an effort-carrying consult event and letting close_event_body run before send. Evidence: read-code: if event_type != "consultation" && event_type != "rating" { return None } - the event is discarded; read-code: details rebuilt key by key from a fixed allowlist; unknown keys never leave, unknown shapes become other/unknown; read-code: Details/RatingDetails structs carry no effort field today. Verify: Add a unit test: a worker event closes to itself; a consultation without effort closes unchanged; run cargo test -p c3 telemetry. Remedy: Extend close_event and the constructors with event_type worker and optional effort fields in one release; state explicitly that missing new fields close to absent/unknown so old clients' events keep flowing.
- **F04-3** [major] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:184`, `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:208`, `crates/c3/src/telemetry/event.rs:58` - The proposed tier score (usefulness per 5-hour-window share) is not computable from published telemetry (the event carries tokens but no quota cost) and is biased where it is computable: the window moved 8->72 % without our runs (another consumer) and 260k tokens moved it 30->65 % (non-linear), so per-run window share is both unattributable and non-monotone in effort. Trigger: Aggregating usefulness per window share at the intake from current event fields. Evidence: read-code: window 8 % -> 72 % between 11:18 and 11:55 WITHOUT our runs; another consumer on the account; read-code: window went 30 -> 65 % for 260k tokens inside one round - non-linear; inferred: quota cost lives only in the ledger (to_quota); the consult event has tokens_in/out but no window share. Verify: RC4: compute per-run window deltas against total window movement on one day of local rollouts and report the attributable fraction. Remedy: Add a bucketed quota_cost to the consult event; score window share only for runs whose delta explains the movement, else mark unscoreable; always publish the token/wall vector so other denominators can be derived later.
- **F04-4** [minor] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:189`, `crates/c3-core/src/effort.rs:103` - Effort tiers are endpoint-local (z.ai: low|high|max with medium->high, xhigh->max; openai: low|medium|high|xhigh). A roster policy written in universal tier names silently overspends on GLM - 'checkpoint medium' is high there - so a per-purpose policy must be expressed in preset names or floor/cap and the SENT tier must be recorded, or the usefulness table compares incomparable tiers. Trigger: Applying the sketch's per-purpose effort policy to glm-5.3 through the caps-v1 mapping. Evidence: read-code: z.ai low|high|max with medium->high and xhigh->max; openai low|medium|high|xhigh; read-code: caps-v1 rows give api.z.ai the zai vocabulary, builtin:openai the openai vocabulary. Verify: Trace one preset (checkpoint -> medium) through the zai mapping and confirm the ledger's effort_sent becomes high. Remedy: Store presets/floor-cap in the roster entry; send effort (post-mapping) and effort_requested (preset) in events; group the usefulness table by sent tier within endpoint vocabulary.
- **F04-5** [minor] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:122`, `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/01-claude-measure-reviewers-and-workers.md:57` - SubagentStop alone yields survivorship-biased and missing-not-at-random data: a worker dying with the host emits no event (error share understated), and the hook carries the agent definition name, not the model or the effort (an explicit Agent-call effort override may be absent from the transcript), so the effort dimension would be systematically empty exactly where it matters. Trigger: Counting tier x effort completion rates from SubagentStop events only. Evidence: read-code: hook fires at agent end with the agent id; tier from the plugin's agent names; effort lives in the definition frontmatter; an Agent-call override 'may not be in the subagent transcript - an open question'; assumed: model and effort presence in the payload is unverified. Verify: RC3: dump the SubagentStart/Stop stdin JSON for one haiku Task with an explicit effort override and inspect which fields exist. Remedy: Pair Start+Stop (orphan Start closed as unknown on next start); record tier, model and effort each with its own source field (hook|transcript|rate|unknown); never let the rate command fill a field the hook did not observe.
- **F04-6** [note] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:149` - The intake already has a free-text agent_report event and the rate command has a --note; the worker event must be a distinct closed class and notes must never leave the machine, or the closed-class contract is broken on the first convenience path. Trigger: Implementing c3 workers rate with a note and reusing the existing intake worker/agent table. Evidence: read-code: intake has agent_report with task_scope free text from an older project; 'the worker event must NOT reuse it'. Verify: Grep the worker-event plan and rate command for any free-text field destined for the intake. Remedy: New event class worker with closed fields only; --note stored locally beside the mark, never serialised into telemetry.
- **F04-7** [note] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:200` - Scout-and-judge fails by compression loss and anchoring: a confident wrong digest misleads the judge worse than no digest, and nothing in the sketch measures that loss. Trigger: Routing a reading pass (GLM low / Haiku) whose digest omits a defect that the judge then never sees. Evidence: read-code: scout writes the digest handoff, the judge gets the digest plus a call cap; no coverage rule or audit arm specified; inferred: an observational usefulness table cannot separate digest loss from tier quality without a raw-diff control arm. Verify: Run 5 judgings with raw diff and 5 with digest on one wave; compare confirmed findings (a pilot of the audit arm). Remedy: Digest as a per-file map with machine-checked coverage and a size cap; judge gets diffstat + file list + limited raw-hunk requests; 1-in-5 raw-diff audit arm; judge cap in calls, not tokens.
- **F04-8** [note] `.collab/consilium-2026-10-14/handoffs/01-claude-measure-reviewers-and-workers.md:52` - Splitting usefulness per reviewer x effort from observational runs confounds tier quality with task difficulty (hard tasks are sent at high effort), so the table will make the expensive tiers look worse; and any derived score published inside the closed vocabulary freezes a definition that will need revision. Trigger: Aggregating the intake's usefulness table per reviewer x effort after months of policy-driven routing. Evidence: read-code: sketch plans a usefulness table split per reviewer x effort and scores a tier by usefulness per window share; inferred: effort is chosen from the purpose, so effort correlates with task class by construction. Verify: Before trusting the table, check within one purpose whether effort assignment varies at all; if not, no identification is possible. Remedy: Add an exploration flag (10-20 % of runs one tier off policy) and/or paired same-brief judgings; publish only raw counts and let nightly derived scores live outside the schema.

