### Findings

- **F08-1** [major] `crates/c3-core/src/quota.rs`, `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:200` - The sketch's denominator (usefulness per 5-hour-window share) cannot compare the two standing consultants and misattributes cost: glm-5.3 has no ChatGPT 5-hour window at all, and Astra's window is shared with the operator's own traffic, so a per-run window-share score charges other consumers to the reviewer and leaves GLM with an undefined denominator. Trigger: Scoring a reviewer on an endpoint with no 5-hour window, or on a window that moved between the run's baseline and its end. Evidence: read-code: The sketch asks how to score a tier as usefulness per 5-hour-window share, not per run.; read-code: The window went 8 to 72 percent between 11:18 and 11:55 with no runs of ours - another consumer on the same ChatGPT account.; read-code: The caps-v1 api.z.ai row declares a zai vocabulary and no window notion; only the builtin openai endpoint has the 5-hour/weekly windows. Verify: List two consecutive Astra rounds from the quota ledger and compare each round's pre-run and post-run window share against the round's own token count; if the window moved by more than the round could explain, per-run share is contaminated. Remedy: Use the marginal window share measured immediately around a run (and a separate correction when an unrelated consumer moves the window); score GLM on money and wall time; only compare reviewers on a cost scale both endpoints have (money or wall time), keeping window share an Astra-internal metric.
- **F08-2** [major] `crates/c3/src/consult/orchestrate.rs:1193`, `crates/c3-core/src/ledger.rs:195`, `crates/c3/src/telemetry/event.rs:58` - An event carrying only the sent `effort` cannot support the reviewer x effort table on z.ai: the zai vocabulary maps both `medium` and `high` to the sent value `high` (and `xhigh` to `max`), so rows with effort=high are an unlabelled blend of two requested tiers - the exact comparison the design exists to make. The ledger already carries the disambiguators (`effort_requested`, `effort_mapping` = zai-v1) but the event drops all of them. Trigger: Splitting the intake's usefulness table by reviewer x effort for glm-5.3, or reading a z.ai `high` row. Evidence: read-code: vocabulary_map: "zai" => ("zai-v1", "low", "high", "high", "max") - medium is sent as high and xhigh as max.; read-code: LedgerEntry already has effort, effort_requested, effort_sent, effort_mapping, effort_caps, effort_confirmed.; read-code: The Details allowlist has engine, provider, model, purpose, outcome, wall_seconds, tokens, findings, flags, panel_size, peers_used, os, runtime, topic_tag, useful, verified, rejected, consult_ref - no effort field. Verify: Read the Details struct in crates/c3/src/telemetry/event.rs and confirm no effort key; then compare the ledger field set with the event field set to see which ledger facts never reach the wire. Remedy: Send `effort` (sent) together with `effort_requested` (the requested tier or preset) and `effort_mapping` (the vocabulary/version label), so a lossy endpoint mapping is recoverable per row.
- **F08-3** [major] `crates/c3/src/telemetry/event.rs:58`, `crates/c3/src/telemetry/classes.rs:267`, `crates/c3-core/src/effort.rs:5` - The closed vocabulary has no version on the wire: CAPS_VERSION (caps-v1) exists only locally, and the event payload is a fixed struct whose exact bytes a unit test pins - so a later caps change silently re-labels historical rows, and any added field is a breaking change for a strict intake. Trigger: A caps-v2 tier set, or an intake that rejects an event containing an unknown key. Evidence: read-code: pub const CAPS_VERSION: &str = "caps-v1" - the version is a local constant with no telemetry counterpart.; read-code: Test a_current_event_closes_to_its_exact_bytes asserts each event serialises to its exact bytes and that whitespace re-normalises to the same bytes.; read-code: Local prior art explicitly ignores unknown keys (no deny_unknown_fields) - the permissive pattern exists but is not stated for the event or the intake. Verify: Ask the operator to show the intake's request validator and confirm whether unknown keys are rejected and whether any schema version is stored per event. Remedy: Add a `vocab_version` to every event and make the intake accept-and-ignore unknown keys before the first new field ships; keep the permissive ingest as the anti-breakage mechanism, not more fields.
- **F08-4** [major] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:118` - The SubagentStop hook alone under-counts the outcome classes the worker table exists to show: it does not fire for a worker the coordinator kills or one that dies with the process, so `stopped` and `error` shares are systematically low, and a worker that never stops is invisible entirely. Trigger: A wave in which the coordinator stops a worker mid-flight, or a worker process dies, the pattern recorded repeatedly in state.md. Evidence: read-code: The plan names stopped-by-coordinator and error as outcome classes while the only signal source given is the SubagentStop hook.; inferred: An end-of-agent hook by construction cannot report an agent that never reaches its end.; assumed: No real SubagentStart/SubagentStop payload was inspected in this session. Verify: Launch one worker, capture its SubagentStart and SubagentStop stdin JSON, then kill a worker mid-flight and observe whether a Stop payload arrives at all. Remedy: Pair SubagentStart with SubagentStop and reconcile at wave end (a Start with no Stop becomes stopped/unknown); carry the worker's effort from the coordinator's own provenance or send `unknown`.
- **F08-5** [major] (no location) - Per-worker attribution from the hook conflates execution with outcome: the hook can report that a worker ran and how it ended, but not whether its result survived, so a worker whose work the coordinator rewrote still scores as reported and flatters its tier. There is no rework class in the proposed vocabulary. Trigger: Any wave where the coordinator rewrites or discards a worker's edit after the worker reports. Evidence: read-code: The proposed outcome classes are completed, error:..., stopped - all execution states; the optional coordinator mark is the only outcome signal and is per worker, only yes/partly/no.; read-code: Observed 2026-10-13/14: workers did each fix in 15-70 minutes and waves were then reworked across multiple rounds, so rework is the normal case.; inferred: The brief itself asks how to attribute a wave's success to a worker when the coordinator reworks it - the vocabulary as sketched cannot. Verify: Take one merged wave and list, per worker, whether the coordinator kept its edit as delivered or rewrote it; count how many would be mis-scored by the current outcome classes. Remedy: Add a coordinator-side post-hoc class `as-delivered | reworked | superseded | unused` per worker, and make the wave/gate the primary attribution unit (did the wave pass, in how many attempts) rather than the individual worker.
- **F08-6** [major] (no location) - Two free-text carriers sit inside the worker and scout designs: the coordinator's `--note` on `c3 workers rate` and the scout's digest. If either is ever placed in a telemetry event (or the digest is stored with telemetry), the privacy-by-construction invariant breaks - the digest is a re-encoding of the brief, paths and code, i.e. exactly what the closed vocabulary exists to keep off the machine. Trigger: Adding a note or digest field to an event, or exporting the digest beyond the local handoff file. Evidence: read-code: The module header states every string an event carries comes from a CLOSED set and that a harmless private label must never leave the machine.; read-code: The worker plan adds a --note text and the scout plan a digest handoff, neither assigned a closed class.; inferred: A digest summarising a diff necessarily contains paths and code fragments, so it cannot travel on the event channel. Verify: Grep the telemetry event constructors and the intake schema for any free-text field (note, digest, title) and confirm none is accepted; check where the digest is written today. Remedy: Keep the digest and the note on the local handoff/ledger channel only, never in an event; pseudonymise every id the way `consult_ref` does; keep `agent_transcript_path` local and delete it per the forget-me contract.
- **F08-7** [major] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:186` - The score the design wants will read noise as signal: with the observed volume (nine Astra rounds in one wave, one animal per reviewer) the reviewer x effort x purpose table has essentially no cells, and the usefulness mark is entered by the same coordinator who chose the tier, so it measures the chooser rather than the tier. A tier policy fitted on this degrades routing. Trigger: Building a per-tier usefulness table from three months of the current cadence, or deriving a roster effort policy from it. Evidence: read-code: The sketch wants the usefulness table split per reviewer x effort while the only evidence quoted is nine rounds of one reviewer at one effort.; read-code: Nine rounds of one wave produced one finding each - a single reviewer-effort cell, n=9, with no control arm.; inferred: Alternative 3 (findings confirmed) is a stronger numerator but needs the findings join, which the brief marks as not yet in place. Verify: Ask the operator for the intake's current reviewer x purpose cell counts and the smallest cell with more than a handful of rows; if every cell is under about twenty, no per-tier score is identifiable. Remedy: Instrument now and defer the policy decision; obtain identified data from a randomized or paired arm (same brief at two tiers, or an epsilon-exploration router arm); report usefulness with the coverage/dispersion, not as a point estimate, and promote confirmed findings once the findings join exists.
- **F08-8** [major] `.collab/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-08/state.md:200` - The scout-and-judge route fails silently at the scout: a cheap tier's false negative is unobservable to the judge, which can only audit the digest, and the digest's incentive is to keep the cheap path short; a byte cap on the digest forces dropping exactly the line anchors that would let the judge contradict it, and a digest that does not name its commit lets the judge reason on a moved tree. Trigger: A diff whose defect is in a hunk the scout summarised as uninteresting, or a judge reading a digest cut at an earlier commit. Evidence: read-code: The sketch routes a cheap tier to write a digest handoff and gives the judge the digest plus a call cap; the digest survives a cold cache, and no coverage or commit anchoring is specified.; read-code: The existing reading discipline already requires `git diff --stat` first, one path at a time with a line cap, and a verdict even on an incomplete read - the discipline that makes a full read auditable.; inferred: Real defects were found in low-salience hunks (an error-to-absence conversion, a second-precision instant), the class of finding a summarising scout is most likely to drop. Verify: Run the scout route on a revision whose defect is already known from a prior full-review round and check whether the digest mentions the hunk at all; repeat on one clean revision to see whether the digest invents findings. Remedy: Cap items and per-item quote length rather than bytes; require one file:line anchor and a short quotable fragment per claim; require a what-I-did-not-read coverage count and the commit/range the digest was cut from; have the judge open at least N anchors itself and get the raw diff of the digest's suspicious hunks; make `digest insufficient, escalate to full read` a first-class counted outcome; and run a periodic unassisted control to measure the scout's miss rate.

